          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The sources I searched do not confirm whether asthma with an acute exacerbation takes one code or a code plus an additional code. I can't answer that from them, so I won't guess.\n\n**What the sources do say:**\n\n- The asthma coding summary (coding-asthma-j45) says asthma is built on two axes, which is why asthma questions \"reduce to two lookups rather than one.\" The passage I retrieved starts mid-sentence, so I can't see what those two lookups are.\n- Exacerbation and status asthmaticus are mutually exclusive. If the record documents status asthmaticus, the code says that and does not also say acute exacerbation. The coder doesn't choose between them. The record's documentation decides.\n- If severity is not documented, asthma goes to the unspecified code. A coder can't infer severity from treatment, peak-flow readings, or other parts of the chart.\n- The COPD summary (coding-copd-j44) says that where asthma is documented alongside COPD, an additional code identifying the type of asthma is added. That instruction belongs to the COPD category, so I can't assume it applies to asthma alone.\n- The diabetes summary (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11) describes a general \"with\" convention, in which the classification presumes a link between two conditions joined by \"with.\" The sources don't say whether that convention applies to asthma with exacerbation.\n\n**What I could not confirm:** The code lookup returned chapter-level context (J00–J99) but not the J45 asthma codes themselves, so I can't give the specific codes or say which one applies. Code validity also changes over time, so check the current ICD-10-CM tabular list and its instructional notes before relying on any code.\n\nIf you are coding a real record, use the current official ICD-10-CM tabular list, or ask a certified coder. If you are or someone you know is having an asthma attack with trouble breathing, get urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
